#
# prima-materia — the Ālvār transmission, entered at the Tiruvāymoḻi
#
# Opened from the structure-driven entrance of SPEC §14: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-indic.ttl with no node. The work opened
# is Nammāḻvār's Tiruvāymoḻi, read whole in one English translation, at the
# hundred terminal stanzas and at every harvested place in the Tamil and in a
# second translation.
#
# Order of the file: the works; the tradition with the three decisions; the
# practices; the capacities named in the body of the work; the yieldings from
# the body; the chains; the cautions; the conditions of failure; the signs of
# success; the protocols; the receiving; the capacities named in the terminal
# stanzas; and last the ninety-three yieldings of the terminal stanzas, decad
# by decad.
#
# Identifiers: Alvar plus the Tamil term in the transliteration of the
# Madras Tamil Lexicon without diacritics. The yieldings of the terminal
# stanzas are named AlvarTvm plus centum and decad plus Payan, the fruit.
#

pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Tiruvāymoḻi"@en, "Tiruvāymoḻi"@de ;
    skos:note "Nammāḻvār, also Caṭakōpaṉ and Māṟaṉ of Kurukūr: a hundred decads of eleven stanzas, one of thirteen, 1102 in all, the fourth part of the Nālāyira Divya Prabandham. The locus used throughout is centum, decad and stanza, which all three texts read here print."@en ;
    skos:note "The Tamil read is the Project Madurai edition, pāsurams 2791 to 3342 of the Nālāyira Divya Prabandham in two files, prepared by K. Kalyanasundaram and first put up in 1998; it gives every stanza with its locus. It does not name the printed edition it was keyed from. Two faults of this text were met: decad 8.10 carries the loci of 9.9 in its later stanzas, and three stanzas of 2.6 and 4.10 lack a locus; every place cited from those decads was located by sequence and content."@en ;
    skos:note "The first translation is Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, The Tiruvaimoli of Nammalvar Rendered in English, Tyaga Bharati Music Education Mission, Melkote 1987, taken from the title page of the Internet Archive full text of the identifier tiruvaimoliofnammalvarrenderedinenglishsriramabharatisowbhagyalakshmi1987ocr. It was read whole. The scan lacks pages 21 to 23, which carry decad 2.1 and the end of 1.10; the errata of the edition confirm the decad is printed there."@en ;
    skos:note "The second translation is S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Tiruvaymoli English Glossary, four volumes, Ananthacharya Indological Research Institute Series V, Bombay 1981, taken from the title page of the Internet Archive full text of the identifier tiruvaymolienglishglossarysatyamurthiayyangargwalior4volumes1981ocr. It prints every stanza in transliteration, a verse translation and notes drawn from the medieval commentaries. It was read at every place harvested and at all hundred terminal stanzas; seven of those it could not be located for, because the verse markers of its text layer are misread, and the nodes say so. Its two thousand pages of notes were not read through."@en ;
    skos:note "The dating is not settled by any of the three texts and is not settled here. The 1987 translation reports that historians place Nammāḻvār around the seventh century and that tradition places his birth at the beginning of the Kali age; the 1981 introduction places the Ālvārs between the sixth and the ninth century. Both say that the work was recovered and set to music by Nāthamuni."@en .

pmw:AlvarNalayiraDivyaPrabandham a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Nālāyira Divya Prabandham"@en, "Nālāyira Divya Prabandham"@de ;
    skos:note "The four thousand stanzas of the twelve Ālvārs, of which the Tiruvāymoḻi is one part and is declared separately. The rest, the works of the other eleven poets and Nammāḻvār's three shorter works, was not opened in this run. It is reachable: the Tamil stands in the same Project Madurai series as the Tiruvāymoḻi, and the translation of Srirama Bharati printed in 2000 as The Sacred Book of Four Thousand lies at the Internet Archive under thesacredbookoffourthousand01nalayiradivyaprabandhamsriramabharati2000 with a text layer."@en .

#
# The tradition, described at its new place
#

pmt:Alvar a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    rdfs:label "Ālvār transmission"@en, "Ālvār-Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Tamil devotional transmission of the twelve Ālvār, in which the relation to the deity is sung in the first person and the states it passes through are named."@en ;
    skos:note "Raised from pm:corpusNamed on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: the whole of the Tiruvāymoḻi in the translation of 1987, all but the one decad its scan lacks; the hundred terminal stanzas in the Tamil and in both translations; and every place a node below cites, in all three. What was not read: Nammāḻvār's other three works and the rest of the Nālāyira Divya Prabandham, and the notes of the 1981 translation beyond the places cited."@en ;
    skos:note "The first of the three decisions SPEC §14 asks for, the contact route, stays at documented. The Tiruvāymoḻi carries the Viṣṇu and Kṛṣṇa lore of the Sanskrit Purāṇas and the epics throughout, names the Vedas as its measure, and is the source of the later Śrīvaiṣṇava commentary that both translations lean on. It therefore counts as no independent witness against the Indian holding, and every agreement between it and a Vaiṣṇava or Vedāntic node elsewhere in the graph is reception until shown otherwise."@en ;
    skos:note "The second decision, recension and edition: the Tamil of the Project Madurai edition, read against two English translations by different translators, Srirama Bharati with Sowbhagya Lakshmi and S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, counted at the translator on the title page. Where they divide, the node records both and the Tamil, and enters on the reading the Tamil supports."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places: 1102 stanzas, of which the hundred terminal stanzas carry the work's promises of fruit and ninety-three of them name one. The work is a single poet's sequence and not a manual: it states few procedures, many fruits, and almost no means of telling whether a fruit came. The count of this file is therefore weighted to the terminal stanzas by the text and not by the harvest."@en ;
    skos:note "What the harvest found, in counts. Six conditions of failure, two of them stated against the work's own practice (worship that is outward, 9.10.7, and calling that is not answered, 4.7) and one stated as the doubt that would follow if the lord did not come (8.1.3), the other three against rival practices; two signs of success, both announced in advance and both read by the tradition's own people; one self-warning to poets about the act the work itself is. Against those, ninety-three promises of fruit for reciting a decad, of which one waives every condition (7.9.11) and none names a sign by which the reciter would know it had come."@en ;
    skos:note "Open, and the next place to go: the states the work names and does not prescribe, the symptoms of separation in decads 2.4, 4.2, 4.4, 5.4, 5.5, 6.5, 7.2 and 9.9, the girl who speaks as the lord in 5.6, and the visions of 7.7 and 8.5, were read and not entered, because they are states undergone and not capacities, cautions, conditions or signs; they are the material the registration named and a harvest of states would take them up. The other works of the corpus are open at pmw:AlvarNalayiraDivyaPrabandham, and the Śrīvaiṣṇava transmission that reads them is registered as pmt:Srivaisnava."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Nālāyira Divya Prabandham, in particular the Tiruvāymoḻi of Nammāḻvār" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli, pmw:AlvarNalayiraDivyaPrabandham ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.1-10.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

#
# The practices, the capacities of the body of the work and their yieldings
#

pmp:AlvarIppattu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Mastering a decad of the Tiruvāymoḻi: reciting, learning or singing its ten stanzas"@en, "Das Beherrschen einer Dekade der Tiruvāymoḻi: ihre zehn Strophen rezitieren, lernen oder singen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "ippattum vallār"@en ;
    skos:definition "Reciting, learning or singing the ten stanzas of one decad of the Tiruvāymoḻi, which the eleventh stanza of the decad names as the act its fruit follows from."@en ;
    skos:note "The terminal stanza of nearly every decad names this act as the condition of its fruit, with a small set of verbs that the two translations render variously as reciting, chanting, learning, mastering and singing: vallār (those able in them), ōtuvār, kaṟpār, pāṭuvār, uraippār, ēttuvār. Some stanzas add a manner, and the manners are entered at the yieldings that carry them: with melody, with love, with insight, daily, by night and by day, until the lisp is gone, with the mind set on it, fully, without abating."@en ;
    skos:note "Of the hundred terminal stanzas, ninety-three name a fruit for the one who masters the decad and seven do not: 1.2.11 and 1.8.11 say only that the decad is the poet's considered word, 2.3.11 and 9.10.11 bid the hearers sing and dance it, 10.6.11 says that it is sweet to the ears of the celestials, 10.7.11 assigns it to the hill of Tirumāliruñcōlai, and 10.3.11 ends in a phrase, avaṟṟiṉ cārvē, that one translation renders as the benefit the speaking cowherdess attained and the other was not located for."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.1.11 and the terminal stanzas of all hundred decads (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarTirunamam a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Uttering the names of the lord"@en, "Das Aussprechen der Namen des Herrn"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "tirunāmam"@en ;
    skos:definition "Saying the lord's names, one or many, aloud or in meditation: Kēcavaṉ, Nāraṇaṉ, Mātavaṉ and the others of the twelve, or the one name nāraṇam that 10.5.1 calls the holy name to be meditated upon."@en ;
    skos:note "Decad 2.7 is built on the twelve names, each stanza beginning with the name that ended the last, and the speaker reports what each name did for him. Decad 10.5 makes one name the mantra, nāraṇam, and 10.2 promises that the obstacles end when Kēcavā is said and that the messengers of death do not come near."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.7.1-13, 10.2.1, 10.2.10 and 10.5.1-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarSankuCakkaramEnral a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Saying conch and discus into the ears of the girl stricken by the god"@en, "Muschel und Diskus in die Ohren des vom Gott ergriffenen Mädchens sagen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "caṅku cakkaram eṉṟu ival kēṭka"@en ;
    skos:definition "The treatment the friend prescribes in decad 4.6 for the girl whose malady is the god himself: to say the words conch and discus clearly so that she hears them, to recite the names of the lord, and to put on her the white dust and the dust of the feet of his devotees."@en ;
    skos:note "Prescribed in the one decad of the work that sets two treatments of the same malady against each other. The friend speaks, the mothers are the ones who have called in the diviner and her rite, and the girl does not speak at all. With the saying of the words go, in the same decad, the recitation of the lord's names, the smearing of white dust and of the dust of the devotees' feet, and praise in company with those who know the Veda."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.6.2-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarVeriyattu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The possession dance and offerings for the minor deity, which the work rejects"@en, "Der Besessenheitstanz und die Gaben für die niedere Gottheit, die das Werk verwirft"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "aṇaṅku āṭutal, veṟi"@en ;
    skos:definition "The treatment the mothers have called in for the stricken girl in decad 4.6: a diviner woman whose word they follow, offerings of flesh and toddy, black and red food laid on an altar, a goat sacrificed, and the women dancing as though possessed and shaking their shoulders to loud music."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because the work records it, as the treatment the mothers reach for first, and because it is the practice against which the decad states a condition of failure. The decad does not name the deity; both translations say only that it is a minor one, and the commentary of 1981 calls the diviner the votary of a deity of a very low order. Who the deity was and how the rite was performed elsewhere is not in this work and is not entered here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.6.3-9 and 4.6.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarPatiAtutal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Singing and dancing the lord's praise in public without shame"@en, "Das schamlose öffentliche Singen und Tanzen zum Lob des Herrn"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "pāṭi āṭi"@en ;
    skos:definition "Singing the lord's glory at the top of one's voice, leaping and dancing, touching the earth with the head, going through the streets and being called mad, with pride and shame given up."@en ;
    skos:note "Decad 3.5 commands it and threatens those who do not do it; decad 2.6 reports it in the first person as what destroyed the speaker's karma."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.6.4-6 and 3.5.1-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarValamVarutal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Going round the lord's shrine"@en, "Das Umschreiten des Heiligtums des Herrn"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "valam ceytal"@en ;
    skos:definition "Circumambulating the temple of the lord, at Tiruvāṟaṉviḷai in 7.10 and at Tirumōkūr in 10.1."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 7.10.8 and 10.1.5-7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarKataittalaiCiykkal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sweeping the threshold of the lord's temple"@en, "Das Fegen der Schwelle des Tempels"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "kaṭaittalai cīykkal"@en ;
    skos:definition "Cleaning the door-step of the temple at Anantapuram."@en ;
    skos:note "The lowest act of service the work names, and the claim attached to it is that even this undoes the deeds that bind: kaṭaittalai cīykkap peṟṟāl kaṭuviṉai kaḷaiyalāmē."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 10.2.7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarArccanai a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Worshipping the lord with flowers, water, lamp and incense"@en, "Die Verehrung des Herrn mit Blumen, Wasser, Lampe und Weihrauch"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "pū, nīr, cuṭar, tīpam"@en ;
    skos:definition "Daily worship at the lord's feet with fresh unfading flowers, water, sandal paste, lamp and incense."@en ;
    skos:note "The work prescribes this worship and in other places sets it aside: 4.3 offers the heart as sandal paste and the poem as garland instead of flowers, and 9.3.9 calls worship with flowers and water, lamp and incense superfluous. The superfluity is not a prohibition, and the prescriptions of 9.10 and 10.2 stand beside it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.3.7, 4.3.1-7, 9.3.9, 9.10.1-10, 10.2.4-10 and 10.5.4-5 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarTontu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Serving the lord's devotees"@en, "Der Dienst an den Verehrern des Herrn"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "toṇṭu, aṭiyār aṭimai"@en ;
    skos:definition "Bonded service to the devotees of the lord and to their servants, down the chain of servants."@en ;
    skos:note "Decads 3.7 and 8.10 put the devotee's devotee above the lord as the object of service, and 3.7.9 says that a devotee of the lowest birth is the speaker's master if he is the lord's."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.7.1-10 and 8.10.1-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarMatalUrtal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Riding the palm-stem horse"@en, "Das Reiten des Palmstängelpferdes"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "maṭal ūrtal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The rejected lover's public act, announced by the speaker of 5.3: to mount the stalk of the palmyra and ride it through every street, inviting the world's slander, so as to shame the lover into coming."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a named practice and as nothing more: the work announces it as a resolve and does not report it carried out, and no fruit is named for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 5.3.9-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarKayamCerral a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conquering the five senses and mortifying the body"@en, "Die fünf Sinne besiegen und den Leib abtöten"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "aimpulaṉ veṉṟu kāyam ceṟṟal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The ascetic's path as the work describes it: cutting off every attachment, conquering the five senses and wasting the body until weeds grow on it."@en ;
    skos:note "A rival path, entered so that the work's condition of failure against it has an object; see pmc:AlvarCuvarkkamMilvar."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.1.9 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarNani a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Contemplating the self apart from all else"@en, "Die Betrachtung des Selbst, abgelöst von allem anderen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "ñāṉi"@en ;
    skos:definition "The path of the knower (ñāṉi) who has given up everything and contemplates the self with sharpened understanding, and attains the enjoyment of the self."@en ;
    skos:note "A rival path, entered so that the work's conditions of failure against it have an object; see pmc:AlvarCirukaNinaivu. The work never names it with the Sanskrit term of the later commentary. Satyamurthi's notes do, and they are commentary and not the stanza."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.1.10, 6.9.10 and 8.8.9 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarEmarEzhuPirappu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Saving one's kin of seven births before and after"@en, "Die Rettung der eigenen Sippe über sieben Geburten davor und danach"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "emar kīḻ mēl ēḻ eḻu piṟappum"@en ;
    skos:definition "The salvation of those connected with the devotee for seven generations above and below: they become devotees of Kēcavaṉ and are kept from the endless hell."@en ;
    skos:note "Claimed twice and both times in the first person and after the fact, not as a promise: the kin are said to have become devotees and to have been spared hell because the speaker sang and named."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.6.7 and 2.7.1 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarKurranTamar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Keeping death's messengers away"@en, "Die Boten des Todes fernhalten"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "kūṟṟiṉ tamarkaḷum kuṟukakillār"@en ;
    skos:definition "The messengers of Yama, who do cruel deeds, do not come near those who say Kēcavā."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 10.2.1 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarAnankuTirttal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The cure of the malady sent by the god"@en, "Die Heilung der vom Gott gesandten Krankheit"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "aṇaṅku, nōy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The recovery of the girl whose sickness is love for the lord: she will recover at once, her fever will subside, and she will rise and dance in worship."@en ;
    skos:note "The malady is diagnosed in the same decad as the work of the supreme god and not of a minor one, and the cure is said to come only from him; the claim therefore makes the diagnosis and the cure one statement, which is what the test at pmc:AlvarAnankuTest has to separate."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.6.1-2, 4.6.5 and 4.6.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTiyaiTaluvi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Embracing fire without being burnt"@en, "Feuer umarmen, ohne zu verbrennen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "cem tīyait taḻuvi mey vēvāḷ"@en ;
    skos:definition "The daughter of decad 4.4 clasps the red fire, calls it Accutaṉ, and her body is not burnt."@en ;
    skos:note "A capacity reported of a person in a state and not acquired by a practice: the mother lists it among the strange deeds of a daughter whom the lord has seized, and no practice in the work is said to produce it. Entered because strict completeness for capacities requires it, and kept apart from the yieldings because it has no pm:byPractice to carry."@en ;
    skos:note "The two translations divide on the verb. Satyamurthi Ayyangar renders the girl as clasping the blistering flame and remaining unhurt; Bharati renders her as fondling the red fire unhurt. Both carry the absence of burning, which is the Tamil mey vēvāḷ, her body does not burn."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.4.3 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTirunamamEmarYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Uttering the names is said to save the speaker's kin of seven births"@en, "Das Aussprechen der Namen soll die Sippe über sieben Geburten retten"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarTirunamam ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarEmarEzhuPirappu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.7.1 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarPatiAtutalEmarYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Constant thought, song and dance are said to have saved the speaker's kin from hell"@en, "Stetes Denken, Singen und Tanzen sollen die Sippe vor der Hölle bewahrt haben"@de ;
    skos:note "Reported in the first person: the speaker thought of the lord constantly and sang and danced, his ageless karma was destroyed, his sickness cured, and his kin of seven generations kept from hell."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarPatiAtutal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarEmarEzhuPirappu, pmc:AlvarVinaiTirtal, pmc:AlvarNoyAruttal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.6.4-7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTirunamamKurranYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saying Kēcavā is said to end every obstacle and keep death's messengers away"@en, "Kēcavā zu sagen soll jedes Hindernis beenden und die Boten des Todes fernhalten"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarTirunamam ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarKurranTamar, pmc:AlvarTuyarIlamai ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 10.2.1 and 10.2.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTirunamamVinaiYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Uttering and singing the names is said to end karma past and to come"@en, "Das Aussprechen und Singen der Namen soll vergangenes und künftiges Karma beenden"@de ;
    skos:note "3.3.6 is the strongest form: to say namas to the lord of Vēṅkaṭam is enough, and the deeds past and to come are ended. Bharati renders it as serving him even only by lip; Satyamurthi Ayyangar as the inclination for selfless service, which shall burn down past sins and those yet to come."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarTirunamam ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarVinaiTirtal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.7.3-9, 3.3.6 and 10.5.7-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTirunamamViduYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Meditating on the name nāraṇam and singing it daily is said to give release"@en, "Die Betrachtung des Namens nāraṇam und sein tägliches Singen sollen Befreiung geben"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarTirunamam ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarVidu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 10.5.1-5 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarSankuCakkaramYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Saying conch and discus into her ears is said to cure the girl at once"@en, "Muschel und Diskus in ihre Ohren zu sagen soll das Mädchen sofort heilen"@de ;
    skos:note "Both translations carry the immediacy: if you will say conch and discus so that she hears, she will recover, see (Bharati: she will immediately recover, just see; Satyamurthi Ayyangar: you will then see it does her real good). The Tamil has naṉṟē il peṟum itu kāṇmiṉē."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarSankuCakkaramEnral ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarAnankuTirttal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.6.2, 4.6.5-6 and 4.6.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarValamVarutalYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Going round the shrine is said to end the woes and the evil deeds"@en, "Das Umschreiten des Heiligtums soll Leid und böse Taten beenden"@de ;
    skos:note "10.1.7 adds a term: the woes will be gone quickly (kaṭitu), once we go round it."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarValamVarutal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarTuyarIlamai, pmc:AlvarVinaiTirtal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 7.10.8 and 10.1.7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarKataittalaiYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sweeping the temple threshold is said to undo the cruel deeds"@en, "Das Fegen der Tempelschwelle soll die grausamen Taten aufheben"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarKataittalaiCiykkal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarVinaiTirtal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 10.2.7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarArccanaiNoyYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Worship of the feet is said to keep disease away and end birth"@en, "Die Verehrung der Füße soll Krankheit fernhalten und die Geburt beenden"@de ;
    skos:note "Satyamurthi Ayyangar's note at 10.4.7 reads the disease as saṃsāra; the Tamil has piṇi, sickness, and piṟavi keṭuttu, ending birth, as two things."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarArccanai ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarNoyAruttal, pmc:AlvarPiravaamai ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 9.10.9 and 10.4.7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarArccanaiViduYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Worship with flowers, water and lamp is said to give release"@en, "Die Verehrung mit Blumen, Wasser und Lampe soll Befreiung geben"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarArccanai ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarVidu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.3.7 and 10.5.5 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTontuVinaiYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Resolving to serve is said to have ended the speaker's evil deeds at once"@en, "Der Entschluss zu dienen soll die bösen Taten des Sprechers sofort beendet haben"@de ;
    skos:note "First person, and with a turn the translations soften: the Tamil says the speaker thought of serving as a pretence (aṭimai ceyvēṉ pōl ... vañcittu), and still every evil path ceased. Bharati: I only thought I would serve the lord, lo, my evil karmas disappeared instantly. The 1981 translation of this stanza was not located."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarTontu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarVinaiTirtal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 8.10.1 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; the Tamil of this decad read at its first stanza, which the Project Madurai text numbers 9.9)" .

#
# The chains, the cautions, the conditions of failure, the signs, the
# protocols and the receiving
#

pmp:AlvarVidumin a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Giving up everything and handing the self to the lord"@en, "Alles aufgeben und das Selbst dem Herrn übergeben"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "vīṭumiṉ muṟṟavum"@en ;
    skos:definition "The renunciation and surrender taught in decad 1.2: to give up everything, to uproot the thought of I and mine, and to direct thought, word and deed to the lord."@en ;
    skos:note "The second decad is the work's one sustained instruction, given in the imperative and in order: give up everything, consider that the life of the body is briefer than lightning, uproot the thought of I and mine, cut attachment, direct thought, word and deed to him, and then wait for the time of leaving the body."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.2.1-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmp:AlvarKavi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Making and singing verse on the lord"@en, "Verse auf den Herrn dichten und singen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "kavi"@en ;
    skos:definition "The poet's craft turned to the lord alone, as the speaker of 3.9 and 10.7 practises it and commends it to other poets."@en ;
    skos:note "The work speaks to poets twice: once to forbid them the praise of men (3.9), once to warn them about what singing the lord does to the singer (10.7.1)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.9.1-11 and 10.7.1-5 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarPacamVituttal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Giving up the bonds to the last"@en, "Das Aufgeben der Bindungen bis zur letzten"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "kaṭaiyaṟap pācaṅkaḷ viṭṭal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The shedding of every attachment (pācam, paṟṟu) without remainder, which two stanzas set before the lord's showing of himself and before reaching him."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.4.10 and 8.2.8 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarKanKotuttal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The lord letting himself be seen"@en, "Dass der Herr sich sehen lässt"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "kāṇkoṭuttal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The lord's showing of himself and of his abodes, Vaikuṇṭha, the milk ocean and the Añjana hill, to the devotee."@en ;
    skos:note "The object of the longing in a large part of the work, and the one outcome the speaker repeatedly reports as not having come; see pmc:AlvarKanaVarai and pmc:AlvarTeruvar."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.7.1-10, 8.1.1-4 and 8.2.8 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarAimpulanVellal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mastering the five senses"@en, "Die Beherrschung der fünf Sinne"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "aimpulaṉ vellal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The conquest of the five senses, which the work names as the obstacle that drags the soul and that the ascetic claims to master."@en ;
    skos:note "Decad 7.1 makes the senses the lord's own snares, set by him, and says that they fell even the gods; mastering them is therefore never presented as the devotee's work alone."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.1.9, 4.7.9 and 7.1.1-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarIlamai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The time before youth and strength fade"@en, "Die Zeit, bevor Jugend und Kraft vergehen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "kiḷaroḷi iḷamai"@en ;
    skos:definition "The span of life before the radiance of youth fades and before old age saps the strength, which two decads set as the time in which to go to the lord's hill."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.10.1 and 3.3.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarPacamPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "He does not show himself until the bonds are given up to the last"@en, "Er zeigt sich nicht, bevor die Bindungen bis zur letzten aufgegeben sind"@de ;
    skos:note "Tamil: kaṭaiyaṟap pācaṅkaḷ viṭṭa piṉṉai aṉṟi avaṉ avai kāṇkoṭāṉē, not unless the bonds are given up to the end does he let them be seen. Bharati: till my last remaining passions leave me, he will not see me. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: these the lord reveals not unless one sheds the last of the attachments. The two translations divide on who does the seeing, the speaker or the lord; both carry the condition and its consequence."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted among the prerequisites and not among the conditions of failure, because the work states it as an order of steps with its consequence and not as a sign that something has failed. It is nevertheless the one prerequisite in the work that a devotee could read as the reason for a failure, and pmc:AlvarKanaVarai shows the speaker doing so with another reason."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:AlvarKanKotuttal ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:AlvarPacamVituttal ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The lord does not let Vaikuṇṭha, the milk ocean and the Añjana hill be seen, and does not show himself, as long as any bond remains." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 8.2.8 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarArulPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The senses are not mastered without the lord's grace"@en, "Ohne die Gnade des Herrn werden die Sinne nicht beherrscht"@de ;
    skos:note "Tamil: eṉṟu yāṉ velkiṟpaṉ uṉ tiruvaruḷ illaiyēl. Bharati: how will I ever control my senses if your grace is not forthcoming. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: but for your sweet grace how can I the senses conquer. A necessity stated as a question and answered by the whole decad, in which the senses are the lord's own snares."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:AlvarAimpulanVellal ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:AlvarArulPeru ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The five, fickle and keeping to no one path, are not conquered: the speaker asks when he could ever conquer them if the lord's grace is not there." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 7.1.7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarPaRRilataPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Who attains the attitude without attachment can reach him"@en, "Wer die Haltung ohne Anhaften erreicht, kann ihn erreichen"@de ;
    skos:note "Tamil: paṟṟilāta pāvaṉai ataṉaik kūṭil avaṉaiyum kūṭalāmē. Bharati: if you can attain detachment, you can reach him too. A sufficient condition stated, with no consequence named for its absence, so prescribed order and not stated necessity."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:AlvarTiruvadi ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:AlvarPacamVituttal ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.4.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarIlamaiPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Go to the lord's hill before youth fades"@en, "Zum Berg des Herrn gehen, bevor die Jugend vergeht"@de ;
    skos:note "2.10.1: before the radiance of youth fades it is wise to reach Māliruñcōlai (Tamil: kiḷaroḷi iḷamai keṭuvataṉ muṉṉam ... cārvatu catirē). 3.3.10: before the numbered days are spent and before old age saps the strength, reach the feet of the lord of Vēṅkaṭam. A term is set and no consequence of missing it is stated."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:AlvarTiruvadi ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:AlvarIlamai ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.10.1 and 3.3.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarViduminPresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "When all is turned to him the obstacles go; then wait for the body to be shed"@en, "Wenn alles ihm zugewandt ist, weichen die Hindernisse; dann auf das Ablegen des Leibes warten"@de ;
    skos:note "Bharati: go to the source of thought, word and deed, direct them to him; when thus directed, all obstacles will vanish, then wait for the moment of shedding the body. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: once you dedicate yourself to him, all your sins will cease. The order is stated and the release placed at the end of the body; no consequence is named for reversing it."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:AlvarVidu ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AlvarVidumin ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.2.8-9 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarIraiNilaiCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Knowing the self apart from the body does not reach the lord"@en, "Das Selbst als vom Leib verschieden zu erkennen, erreicht den Herrn nicht"@de ;
    skos:note "Bharati: even if you realise your nature as different from body, formless, without length, breadth or height, the lord is not attained. The Tamil: uṇarntu uṇarntu ... uṇarilum iṟai nilai uṇarvaritu, though one understand and understand again, the state of the lord is hard to understand. The 1981 translation of this stanza was not located; what was located there is its preamble."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AlvarNani ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.3.6 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987)" .

pmc:AlvarNaniCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The joy of the self, for endless ages, does not match a short while of service"@en, "Die Freude des Selbst, endlose Zeitalter lang, wiegt kurzen Dienst nicht auf"@de ;
    skos:note "Tamil: kuṟukā nīḷā ... aḷavil iṉpam cērntālum ... uṉakkē āḷākum ciṟu kālattai uṟumō. Bharati: even if I were to attain the infinite pleasures of the self for ages, will that ever match even a short while of service to you without returns. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: the so-called bliss in disembodied state on final emancipation is eternal and changeless, but can it ever match the bliss of service at your feet even once and only for a short time."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AlvarNani ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 6.9.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarYokikalCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The yogis who make their own release and roam"@en, "Die Yogis, die sich ihre eigene Befreiung machen und umherziehen"@de ;
    skos:note "The translations divide and the division is the finding. Bharati: it is not hard to see men wandering in self-made heavens; such yogis are on earth, they have been and will be. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: it will be a notion blind that the soul could itself become the lord; such perverts exist at all times. The Tamil, vīṭaip paṇṇi ... ōṭit tiriyum yōkikaḷum uḷarum, has yogis who make release and run about, and keeps the lord and the soul apart in the line before. Both translations make it a warning against a rival account of release; they differ on whether its error is a false heaven or a false identity."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AlvarNani ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 8.8.9 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarPatmanapanCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Even in confusion do not let go of Padmanābha"@en, "Selbst in Verwirrung Padmanābha nicht loslassen"@de ;
    skos:note "Tamil: maruṭiyēlum viṭēl kaṇṭāy nampi paṟpanāpaṉaiyē. Bharati: not even through oversight must you stop chanting Padmanabha. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: shrink not from him still, unto Patpanapan hold on. Addressed by the speaker to his own heart."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AlvarTirunamam ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.7.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarPatatarCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Those who do not sing and dance are born again and fall to hell"@en, "Wer nicht singt und tanzt, wird wiedergeboren und fällt in die Hölle"@de ;
    skos:note "Tamil 3.5.2: paṇkaḷ talaikkoḷḷap pāṭip paṟantum kuṉittu uḻalātār maṇkoḷ ulakil piṟappār. Bharati: those who cannot sing on top throat and dance in ecstasy must forever suffer the throes of karmic birth; 3.5.3 adds stormy hell for those who do not dance and touch the earth with their heads. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: will in this sinful world get trapped, again and again. A warning with a consequence, aimed at those who omit the practice; it states no consequence for those who perform it badly."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AlvarPatiAtutal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.5.2-4 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarKaviCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Poets, beware as you sing: he enters as the poet and eats heart and life"@en, "Dichter, gebt acht beim Singen: er kommt als der Dichter und verzehrt Herz und Leben"@de ;
    skos:note "The work's warning about itself. Tamil: cem col kavikāḷ uyir kāttu āṭceymiṉ, poets of fine words, guard your life as you do service; the lord of Tirumāliruñcōlai, a deceiving thief, came as a wondrous poet, mingled with heart and life, and ate them, and became the speaker himself. Bharati: be on your guard when you sing. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: beware, ye poets, better be all attention as you sing, else you can't subsist."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning in the strict sense SPEC §15 asks for: the danger named is the one the text itself has undergone and commends, and the stanza gives no way to sing the lord without incurring it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AlvarKavi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 10.7.1 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarManitamCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Poets, do not sing the praise of men"@en, "Dichter, singt nicht das Lob von Menschen"@de ;
    skos:note "Bharati 3.9.4: what do you get, and how long does it last; 3.9.9: I was not born to sing in praise of mortal man. The caution comes with its counter-promise, that the lord will provide for all one's lives, and that promise is entered as a yielding of its own only in so far as the terminal stanza carries it (pmc:AlvarTvm0309Payan)."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AlvarKavi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.9.1-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarVeriyattuCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Do not call in the diviner and her rite for the stricken girl"@en, "Für das ergriffene Mädchen nicht die Wahrsagerin und ihren Ritus rufen"@de ;
    skos:note "Bharati 4.6.3: do not go and do something wild, throwing flesh and toddy, heed not this strange gypsy's words; 4.6.8: this is lowly. Satyamurthi Ayyangar 4.6.3: listen not to this gipsy, you'd better desist from offering meat and liquor. The caution has a stated consequence, which is entered apart as the condition of failure pmc:AlvarAnankuPerukum."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AlvarVeriyattu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.6.3-9 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarAimpulanCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The five senses are snares set by the lord, and they fell even the gods"@en, "Die fünf Sinne sind vom Herrn gelegte Schlingen, und sie stürzen selbst die Götter"@de ;
    skos:note "The speaker complains that the lord has set five tyrants to rule him, planted them as snares, made them a wheel that whirls him into incurable sickness; 7.1.6 and 7.1.9 say that they afflict and fell even the celestials. The caution is about the goal and not about a practice: nothing the devotee does is said to be safe from them."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:AlvarTiruvadi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 7.1.1-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarAlutalCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Kings who ruled the world go begging; their rule does not last"@en, "Könige, die die Welt beherrschten, gehen betteln; ihre Herrschaft hat keinen Bestand"@de ;
    skos:note "The same work that promises sovereignty over earth and heaven as the fruit of a decad (3.10.11, 4.3.11, 6.7.11, 9.8.11) opens its fourth hundred by showing what sovereignty comes to: monarchs who ruled the world as one empire go begging, leg bitten by a black bitch, bowl broken; their forts are razed; their queens are enjoyed by others. Satyamurthi Ayyangar renders the same sequence. The caution and the promise stand side by side and the work does not reconcile them; it says only that the lasting rule is the one at the lord's feet."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:AlvarAlutal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.1.1-8 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarPurameTozuvar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "He is false to those who worship outwardly"@en, "Er ist falsch zu denen, die äußerlich verehren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition of failure the work states for worship as such: the lord of Tirukkaṇṇapuram is true (meyyaṉ) to all who worship him with desire and false (poyyaṉ) to all who worship from outside (puṟamē), and near to those who hold him in their hearts."@en ;
    skos:note "Bharati: he is true to all who seek with love and false to those who worship outwardly. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: his true nature he does conceal from them that worship him for achieving their personal needs. The translations divide on what outward means, a worship of the surface or a worship for other ends; both make the same act of worship fail by the worshipper's inner state, which no one but the worshipper can inspect. That is why it is a condition of failure and why it makes the claims it governs hard to fail: any worship that brought nothing can be assigned to it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 9.10.7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarKanaVarai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "He is called by every name and does not come"@en, "Er wird bei jedem Namen gerufen und kommt nicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one place where the work reports at length a practice performed and its result not following: the speaker stands with hands on his head and calls the lord by name, Nārāyaṇa, the one who swallowed the world, the icon of knowledge, by night and by day and at all times, and the lord neither shows his form nor calls him; and the speaker names his own lacks as the cause."@en ;
    skos:note "The cause is stated in 4.7.9 and the translations differ in what they let it say. Tamil: iṭakilēṉ oṉṟu aṭṭakilēṉ aimpulaṉ vellakilēṉ ... pūppaṟittu ēttakilēṉ. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: alms I gave not, nor did I allay the thirst of others, the senses five I kept not under restraint, flowers I offered not at the appropriate time. Bharati: I have no good-will, no riches, no power over my senses, nor steadfast devotion to worship you with flowers. In 4.7.1 and 4.7.3 the speaker adds his low birth and dark indelible deeds."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure because it names an outcome that did not come and the reason given for it. The decad itself does not end in failure: 4.7.7 reports seeing him as an icon of knowledge in the depth of the soul, and the next decads report union. The non-appearance is a stage, and the work treats it as one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.7.1-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTeruvar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "If he does not come again, how will his devotees be convinced"@en, "Wenn er nicht wieder kommt, wie sollen seine Verehrer überzeugt werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work names the outcome that would count against its own claim and names it as such: if the lord does not come today in a form, how will his people be convinced (eṅṅaṉam tēṟuvar umarē), and the speaker adds that he too may begin to doubt that the lord appears before devotees in the forms they worship and accepts their offerings."@en ;
    skos:note "Bharati 8.1.3: if you do not appear again, how will devotees believe in you; 8.1.4: then I too may doubt that you appear before devotees in forms that they worship and accept their offerings; 8.1.7: alas, I too may begin to doubt that you are all these. The 1981 translation of 8.1.3 was located and its rendering of the last line is cut off in the text layer; it was not read."@en ;
    skos:note "The rarest thing in a devotional corpus and the reason this node exists: the claim that the lord appears to devotees is set, by the devotee, against the case of his not appearing, and the doubt is spoken rather than forbidden. The work does not draw the conclusion; it calls again."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 8.1.3-7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987)" .

pmc:AlvarCuvarkkamMilvar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ascetic reaches heaven and comes back"@en, "Der Asket erreicht den Himmel und kehrt zurück"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's condition of failure for the ascetic path: those who cut all attachments, conquer the five senses and mortify the body reach the sweet heaven and return from it; there is no return for those who reach the feet of the lord with the bird on his banner."@en ;
    skos:note "Tamil: ceṭi maṉṉu kāyam ceṟṟārkaḷum āṅku avaṉai illār ... iṉ cuvarkkam eytiyum mīḷvarkaḷ, mīḷvu illai. Bharati: they enjoy heaven and return. The 1981 translation of this stanza was not located in its text layer."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure stated by one path against another. It names a definite outcome, the return, and so could be failed, but the outcome is placed after death in both cases."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.1.9 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987)" .

pmc:AlvarCirukaNinaivu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "For the knower a small thought becomes a bond, and there is no release"@en, "Für den Wissenden wird ein kleiner Gedanke zur Bindung, und es gibt keine Befreiung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's condition of failure for the path of the knower who contemplates the self: even for him who has given up everything with sharpened understanding, if that fruit is not had, a bond arises from a small thought, and then there is no release; release is to hold to the lord and not let go."@en ;
    skos:note "Tamil: iṟukal iṟappu eṉṉum ñāṉikkum ap payaṉ illaiyēl ciṟuka niṉaivatu ōr pācam uṇṭām piṉṉum vīṭillai. Bharati: seers who contemplate on consciousness do attain the heaven of Atman but memory remains, and brings them back to passion, then there is no liberation. The commentary of the 1981 translation, located in its notes on the decad, illustrates the small thought with Jaḍabharata, whose care for a pet deer at the moment of death undid him; the illustration is the commentator's and not the stanza's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.1.10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987)" .

pmc:AlvarAnankuPerukum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "With the possession rite the fever does not subside but grows"@en, "Mit dem Besessenheitsritus sinkt das Fieber nicht, sondern steigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition of failure the decad states for the rival treatment: while the mothers dance as though possessed, the fever does not abate, the sickness does not leave her, it only grows; nothing but the dust of the feet of the lord's people will do for this malady."@en ;
    skos:note "Tamil: taṇiyum poḻutillai nīr aṇaṅku āṭutir aṉṉaimīr piṇiyum oḻikiṉṟatillai perukum itu allāl. Bharati: her fever will only increase and not subside. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: it does only aggravate the malady. The commentary of 1981 adds two anecdotes, a devotee revived by a name whispered into his ears and a village deity whose tortures redoubled when it was served with a devotee's belongings; both are commentary and not stanza."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure with an observable outcome and a short term, which is rare in this work; it is stated against the rival rite, and its mirror, the immediate recovery under the prescribed treatment, is what pmc:AlvarAnankuTest tests."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.6.6 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarAtaiyalam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The sign: her lips have taken the red of the areca of Tiruppuliyūr"@en, "Das Zeichen: ihre Lippen haben das Rot der Arekanuss von Tiruppuliyūr angenommen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sign by which the mother of decad 8.9 knows that her daughter has received the lord's favour: the girl's lips have acquired the red of the areca fruit that grows by his grace at Tiruppuliyūr; with it go the joy in her face and jewels and dress whose origin cannot be accounted for."@en ;
    skos:note "The work's own word is aṭaiyāḷam, sign or mark: tiruvaruḷkaḷum cērntamaikku aṭaiyāḷam tiruntavuḷa. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: this is a sure sign indeed that she remains immersed in the sweet grace of Kaṇṇapirāṉ. Bharati: there is clear proof that the slender one has received the favours and the grace of the lord."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted on the sign side and apart from the conditions of failure, per SPEC §15. Read by an observer and not reported by the subject, which is unusual; but the observer reads it as she has been told to read the decad, and the sign is the colour of a fruit of the lord's own town, so that it confirms by construction."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 8.9.5-6 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTulavamKamalum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The sign: the body smells of the lord's tulasi"@en, "Das Zeichen: der Leib duftet nach dem Tulasi des Herrn"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sign named three times and once in the first person: the person who has had the lord's favour smells of the tulasi of his garland. The mother of 8.9 asks what other cause there could be for her daughter's fragrance; the mother of 4.4 smells it on her daughter among the signs of her seizure; and the speaker of 10.6.7 says that, having reached the lord's feet, his own body gives off the scent of the tulasi on them."@en ;
    skos:note "Tamil 8.9.10: aṉṟi maṟṟōr upāyam eṉ ival am taṇ tuḻāy kamaḻtal. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: the aroma of tulasi on this lady reveals her contact gracious with the wondrous lord; what other reason can I for this tell. Tamil 10.6.7: viraikuḻuvum naṟum tuḷavam mey niṉṟu kamaḻumē. Both translations carry the first-person stanza."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted with pmc:AlvarAtaiyalam as one of the two signs of success in the work, against six conditions of failure. Like it, it is announced by the tradition before it is observed, and nobody in the work smells for it where the lord's favour was not supposed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 8.9.10, 4.4.3 and 10.6.7 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarAnankuTest a pm:Testing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    rdfs:label "The immediate recovery the decad promises against the growing fever it predicts"@en, "Die sofortige Genesung, die die Dekade verspricht, gegen das steigende Fieber, das sie vorhersagt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol the decad states in both directions and with a short term: the girl stricken by the lord recovers at once when conch and discus are said into her ears, and her fever grows under the possession rite. The decad names the treatment, the observable and the term, and it names the outcome under the rival treatment."@en ;
    skos:note "What weakens it is the diagnosis. The decad cures only a malady it has already ascribed to the supreme god, and a girl who did not recover could be said not to have had that malady; the commentary's anecdotes show the move made in the other direction. A protocol would have to fix the diagnosis before the treatment, by the decad's own signs, and the decad gives them: the girl speaks only of the lord, asks for his tulasi, and wastes."@en ;
    skos:note "No case series exists and none is proposed here."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:AlvarSankuCakkaramYielding ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A girl in the condition the decad describes, to whom conch and discus are said audibly and the names recited, who does not recover at once; or one treated with the rival rite whose fever subsides. Either falls due against the decad as stated." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 4.6.1-10 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarNoyTest a pm:Testing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    rdfs:label "The cure of disease promised to the reciters of two decads"@en, "Die Heilung von Krankheit, die den Rezitierenden zweier Dekaden versprochen ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two terminal stanzas promise the cure of disease (nōy) to those who master the decad, with the ordinary word for sickness and without a term."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-reading stands in the second translation and is the reason this protocol is weak before it starts. Satyamurthi Ayyangar's commentary reads the disease as saṃsāra elsewhere in the work (10.4.7), and a reading of nōy as saṃsāra makes the claim one that no living reciter could fail. The stanzas themselves say nōy, the word the work also uses for the girl's sickness in 4.6."@en ;
    skos:note "No case series exists and none is proposed here."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:AlvarTvm0107Payan, pmc:AlvarTvm0907Payan ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A reciter who has mastered decad 1.7 or 9.7 and is sick, and who remains sick. The stanzas state the cure without condition of manner and without delay, so a reciter who stays sick falls due against them; the stanzas give no term, so a protocol would have to set one." ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.7.11 and 9.7.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTanSollalTan a pm:Receiving ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    rdfs:label "The lord sang his own praise through Caṭakōpaṉ's words"@en, "Der Herr sang sein eigenes Lob durch die Worte Caṭakōpaṉs"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work states of itself that its speaker is not its author: the lord entered the poet, made it seem that he sang with his own words, and with words of his own sang his own praise; the poet uttered empty words and the lord filled them with meaning; and he chose this poet and not great poets of merit."@en ;
    skos:note "Tamil 7.9.2: eṉ collāl yāṉ coṉṉa iṉkavi eṉpittu taṉ collāl tāṉ taṉṉaik kīrttitta māyaṉ. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: who would from within me dictate and sing his glory in words this mean vassal would repeat, bring out this hymnal and, as its author, make me out. Bharati: making it appear like I was singing with words mine, he with words his, has sung his praise. The claim returns at 10.7.2 and 10.7.5, where the lord of Tirumāliruñcōlai became the speaker and praised himself in songs set to paṇ through him."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded as a claim the work makes about itself and not as its source. The citations of this file name Nammāḻvār as the author because that is what a reader can find; who spoke the words is a question this node makes addressable and does not answer."@en ;
    pm:receivedBy "Caṭakōpaṉ of Kurukūr, called Māṟaṉ, who names himself in the terminal stanzas" ;
    pm:claimedSpeaker "The lord, the first cause, who is said to have entered the speaker's speech and sung his own praise in it" ;
    pm:receptionCircumstance "Stated as an inward taking-over and not as a dictation: the lord entered the poet, became him and sang through his tongue; there is no scene, no hearer and no scribe, and the work gives no circumstance beyond the poet being made the lord's own." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 7.9.1-10, 10.7.1-5 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

#
# The capacities named in the terminal stanzas
#

pmc:AlvarVidu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Release"@en, "Befreiung"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "vīṭu"@en ;
    skos:definition "Release from bondage, named in the terminal stanzas with vīṭu and uyyal and once as the soul rid of its body of flesh. The text does not say in these stanzas what is released into; elsewhere it names the feet of the lord and service at them as the goal."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 1.1.11, 2.9.11, 3.2.11, 3.4.11, 3.10.11, 8.1.11, 9.9.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.1.11, 2.9.11, 3.2.11, 3.4.11, 3.10.11, 8.1.11 and others (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarVaikuntam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reaching Vaikuṇṭha or heaven"@en, "Das Erreichen Vaikuṇṭhas oder des Himmels"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "vaikuntam, vāṉ, vicumpu"@en ;
    skos:definition "Ascent to the lord's world, named as Vaikuṇṭha, the high heaven or the shining sky, in several stanzas after death and in one as the city of no return; in one the reciter's own town becomes Vaikuṇṭha."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 1.4.11, 2.1.11, 2.5.11, 2.8.11, 3.8.11, 4.4.11, 4.7.11, 4.8.11, 4.10.11, 5.3.11, 5.4.11, 5.10.11, 6.10.11, 8.4.11, 8.6.11, 9.3.11, 10.8.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.4.11, 2.1.11, 2.5.11, 2.8.11, 3.8.11, 4.4.11 and others (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarPiravaamai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Not being born again"@en, "Nicht wiedergeboren werden"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "piṟavāmai, caṉmam ceyyāmai"@en ;
    skos:definition "The end of rebirth, stated with piṟavār, piṟappu aṟukkum and caṉmam muṭiyum."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 1.3.11, 1.6.11, 2.10.11, 3.1.11, 3.7.11, 3.9.11, 4.8.11, 8.3.11, 8.4.11, 8.7.11, 9.6.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.3.11, 1.6.11, 2.10.11, 3.1.11, 3.7.11, 3.9.11 and others (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarNoyAruttal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The cure of disease"@en, "Die Heilung von Krankheit"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "nōy aṟuttal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The driving off and cutting of disease (nōy), promised to the reciter in two terminal stanzas and to worship in the body of the work. The commentary of the 1981 translation reads the disease as saṃsāra; the stanzas themselves say nōy, the ordinary word for sickness."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 1.7.11, 9.7.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.7.11, 9.7.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTuyarIlamai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Freedom from affliction"@en, "Freiheit von Leid"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "tuyar, iṭar, tukkam, parivu"@en ;
    skos:definition "The absence of affliction and misery for the reciter, named with parivu, āḻtuyar, tukkam and iṭar."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 1.5.11, 4.1.11, 4.6.11, 10.1.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.5.11, 4.1.11, 4.6.11, 10.1.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarVinaiTirtal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ending of karma"@en, "Das Enden des Karma"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "viṉai tīrtal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The removal of viṉai, the deeds that bind, rendered as karma by one translator and as sin by the other; once said to be reduced to ash."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 3.5.11, 4.4.11, 4.5.11, 7.1.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.5.11, 4.4.11, 4.5.11, 7.1.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarAtimai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Service to the lord and his devotees"@en, "Dienst am Herrn und an seinen Verehrern"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "aṭimai"@en ;
    skos:definition "Becoming fit for and placed in aṭimai, bonded service, to the lord or to his devotees; the work treats this as the goal and in several stanzas ranks it above release."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 2.4.11, 5.6.11, 6.5.11, 6.9.11, 7.3.11, 8.9.11, 8.10.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.4.11, 5.6.11, 6.5.11, 6.9.11, 7.3.11, 8.9.11 and others (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTiruvadi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reaching the lord's feet"@en, "Das Erreichen der Füße des Herrn"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "tiruvaṭi, tāḷ, kaḻal"@en ;
    skos:definition "Being brought to the feet of the lord, the fruit most often named after Vaikuṇṭha."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 1.9.11, 2.7.13, 2.10.11, 4.9.11, 5.1.11, 8.8.11, 9.2.11, 9.4.11, 10.4.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.9.11, 2.7.13, 2.10.11, 4.9.11, 5.1.11, 8.8.11 and others (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarAlutal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rule over earth or heaven"@en, "Herrschaft über Erde oder Himmel"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "āḷutal"@en ;
    skos:definition "Sovereignty, over the earth, over heaven or over both, promised as fruit of the recitation."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 2.8.11, 3.10.11, 4.3.11, 6.7.11, 9.8.11, 10.8.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.8.11, 3.10.11, 4.3.11, 6.7.11, 9.8.11, 10.8.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarNalkuravuIlamai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Freedom from want"@en, "Freiheit von Mangel"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "nalkuravu illai, ūṉam illai"@en ;
    skos:definition "The absence of want, defect and poverty (ūṉam, nalkuravu); one translator brackets the poverty as poverty of love for God."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 2.2.11, 6.2.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.2.11, 6.2.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarKalvi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge and clarity of mind"@en, "Wissen und Klarheit des Geistes"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "kalvi, teḷivu"@en ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge (kalvi) for the one who learns without abating, and clarity of mind (teḷivu)."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 1.10.11, 7.5.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.10.11, 7.5.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarUlamMacuArutal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The cleansing of the heart"@en, "Die Reinigung des Herzens"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "uḷḷattu mācu aṟuttal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The cutting away of the stain of the heart."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 5.2.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 5.2.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarUrukutal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The melting of the heart"@en, "Das Schmelzen des Herzens"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "urukutal"@en ;
    skos:definition "The melting of the reciter or listener like fine sand in a spring; the commentary counts the melting itself as the benefit."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 6.8.11, 9.5.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 6.8.11, 9.5.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarVenri a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Success"@en, "Erfolg"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "veṉṟi"@en ;
    skos:definition "Victory or success, unspecified, for those who learn the decad."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 7.4.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 7.4.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarInpam a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Joy and bliss"@en, "Freude und Seligkeit"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "iṉpam, makiḻ"@en ;
    skos:definition "Joy, in one stanza in this birth and in sight of others, in another however the decad is sung."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 7.2.11, 7.8.11, 7.9.11, 8.2.11, 8.5.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 7.2.11, 7.8.11, 7.9.11, 8.2.11, 8.5.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarMatantaiyar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Being desired by women"@en, "Von Frauen begehrt werden"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "maṭantaiyar"@en ;
    skos:definition "Being loved, fanned or embraced by women, on earth or in the celestial world; one commentary reads the women as the lord's devotees."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 5.8.11, 6.1.11, 7.6.11, 10.2.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 5.8.11, 6.1.11, 7.6.11, 10.2.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarVanavar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Standing with the celestials"@en, "Stand bei den Himmlischen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "vāṉavar, amarar"@en ;
    skos:definition "Being fit company for, dear to, gurus to or praised by the celestials."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 1.3.11, 3.4.11, 4.2.11, 5.7.11, 6.3.11, 6.6.11, 7.2.11, 7.7.11, 7.10.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.3.11, 3.4.11, 4.2.11, 5.7.11, 6.3.11, 6.6.11 and others (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTamar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Becoming the lord's devotee"@en, "Zum Verehrer des Herrn werden"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "tamar, pattar"@en ;
    skos:definition "Joining the lord's own people (tamar) or becoming a devotee (pattar, vaiṭṭaṇavar)."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 2.6.11, 3.6.11, 5.5.11, 6.4.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 2.6.11, 3.6.11, 5.5.11, 6.4.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarPukal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fame and standing among men"@en, "Ruhm und Stand unter den Menschen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "pukaḻ"@en ;
    skos:definition "Praise of the world, distinction and an exalted birth."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 3.3.11, 5.9.11, 9.1.11, 10.10.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 3.3.11, 5.9.11, 9.1.11, 10.10.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarMunivar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Becoming a sage"@en, "Zum Weisen werden"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "muṉivar"@en ;
    skos:definition "Becoming muṉivar, sages, in the last decad but one."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 10.9.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 10.9.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarArulPeru a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Receiving the lord's grace"@en, "Die Gnade des Herrn empfangen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "aruḷ pēṟu"@en ;
    skos:definition "The gain of grace, as the poet received it."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as a fruit of reciting a decad in the terminal stanzas 10.5.11."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 10.5.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

#
# The ninety-three yieldings of the terminal stanzas
#

pmc:AlvarTvm0101Payan a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Tiruvāymoḻi 1.1.11: the decad is said to yield release"@en, "Tiruvāymoḻi 1.1.11: Frucht der Dekade: Befreiung"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarIppattu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarVidu ;
    skos:note "Tamil: vīṭē. Bharati: offers liberation to those who recite it. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: shall lead the chanters to final emancipation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.1.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTvm0103Payan a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Tiruvāymoḻi 1.3.11: the decad is said to yield freedom from rebirth and standing with the celestials"@en, "Tiruvāymoḻi 1.3.11: Frucht der Dekade: Nicht wiedergeboren werden und Stand bei den Himmlischen"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarIppattu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarPiravaamai, pmc:AlvarVanavar ;
    skos:note "Tamil: vallār amararōṭu uyarvil ceṉṟu aṟuvar tam piṟavi am ciṟai. Bharati: those who master it will rejoice in heaven. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: those conversant with them will go up with the immortals and cut the prison of their birth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.3.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTvm0104Payan a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Tiruvāymoḻi 1.4.11: the decad is said to yield reaching Vaikuṇṭha or heaven"@en, "Tiruvāymoḻi 1.4.11: Frucht der Dekade: Das Erreichen Vaikuṇṭhas oder des Himmels"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarIppattu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarVaikuntam ;
    skos:note "Tamil: peṟalākum vāṉ ōṅku peruvaḷamē. Bharati: those who master it shall enjoy the wealth of heaven. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: will attain the supreme bliss of service in heaven."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Alvar ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AlvarTiruvaymoli ;
    dcterms:source "Nammāḻvār, Tiruvāymoḻi 1.4.11 (Tamil, Project Madurai ed.; trans. Srirama Bharati and Sowbhagya Lakshmi, Melkote 1987; trans. S. Satyamurthi Ayyangar, Bombay 1981)" .

pmc:AlvarTvm0105Payan a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Tiruvāymoḻi 1.5.11: the decad is said to yield freedom from affliction"@en, "Tiruvāymoḻi 1.5.11: Frucht der Dekade: Freiheit von Leid"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AlvarIppattu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AlvarTuyarIlamai ;
    skos:note "Tamil: vallārkku illai parivatē. Bharati: those who sing it will never suffer on earth. Satyamurthi Ayyangar: shall for ever be free from affliction."@en ;
